<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="trans-zeatin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-2681" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Cytokinins play important physiological roles in plants, from promotion of cell division, to release of lateral buds from apical dominance or delay of senescence. Although their biological activity, signal transduction and metabolism have long been studied, much remains to be uncovered of their genetics and biosynthesis |CITS: [11337393]|. The natural cytokinins are adenine derivatives and can be classified by the configuration of their &lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-side chain as either isoprenoid or aromatic cytokinins. The most prevalent cytokinins by far are those with an unsaturated isoprenoid side chain, and in particular those with a &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-hydroxylated &lt;i&gt;N&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/i&gt;-side chain, such as |FRAME: CPD-4210| and its derivatives. In terms of biological activity, it would seem that, at least in the plant |FRAME: TAX-3702|, two cytokinin species: |FRAME: CPD-4210| (&lt;i&gt;t&lt;/i&gt;Z) and |FRAME: CPD-4209| (iP), are considered to be active forms. By contrast, |FRAME: CPD-4441| (&lt;i&gt;c&lt;/i&gt;Z) exhibits only low or no activity in this plant. It has been suggested that |FRAME: CPD-4441| might contribute to the synthesis of |FRAME: CPD-4210| through interconversion by a zeatin &lt;i&gt;cis-trans&lt;/i&gt;-isomerase |CITS:[12231873]|. The biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-4441| is described in |FRAME: PWY-2781|. Multiple routes have been proposed for the biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-4210|. One pathway is described here. A second, tRNA pathway has been proposed based on the discovery that some tRNA molecules contain an |FRAME: CPD-4207| (iPA) residue at the site adjacent to the anticodon |CITS:[402938]|. The modification is catalyzed by an enzyme that has been identified in various organisms including |FRAME:TAX-562| |CITS: [4346747]|, |FRAME:TAX-4932| |CITS: [4311031]|, |FRAME:TAX-1579| |CITS: [809333]|, |FRAME:TAX-9606| |CITS: [11111046]| and |FRAME:TAX-4577| |CITS: [342386]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The pathway thought to produce the majority of &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; cytokinins utilizes |FRAME: CPD-4211| and AMP, ADP or ATP as substrates, and proceeds via the intermediate |FRAME: CPD-4205|. The isopentenyl group of |FRAME: CPD-4211| is transferred to the N&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt; of AMP, ADP or ATP, resulting in the production of |FRAME: CPD-4205|, |FRAME: CPD-4203| and |FRAME: CPD-4201|, respectively. These reactions are catalyzed by a single enzyme, |FRAME: AT1G68460-MONOMER|. In |FRAME: TAX-3702| seven IPT genes have been identified |CITS:[11313355][11479373][12734720]|. It has been shown that an enzyme encoded by the |FRAME: G-12015| gene removes the phosphoribosyl group from |FRAME: CPD-4205|, producing |FRAME: CPD-4209|, which can be converted to the active |FRAME: CPD-4210| by a single hydroxylation reaction |CITS: [17287810]|. In addition, another hydroxylase in known to act on |FRAME: CPD-4205|, |FRAME: CPD-4203| and |FRAME: CPD-4201|, converting them to &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-zeatin ribosides |CITS:[15280363]|. Following the removal of the phosphate groups from the di- and tri-phosphate forms, the product, |FRAME: CPD-4206|, is converted to the active form by the |FRAME: G-12015|-encoded phosphoribohydrolase |CITS: [17287810]|. Two possible sources exist for the prenyl group: the |FRAME: PWY-922 &quot;mevalonate pathway&quot;| (MVA) and the |FRAME: NONMEVIPP-PWY| (MEP). In |FRAME: TAX-3702|, the plastidic MEP pathway is at the origin of the |FRAME: CPD-4210| species, whereas the |FRAME: CPD-4441| derivatives use the cytosolic MVA pathway |CITS: [14726522]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
